# PLACEHOLDER art for hemp_canvas. Warlon Mhite intends to redraw this.
#
# A square of cloth pulled taut by its four corners, not a rectangle of weave. The old version
# was a 14x14 slab with hard vertical sides, which at 16px reads as a missing texture rather
# than as fabric. This one is 12x12 and squarish -- but the four corners are the only points
# that reach the bounding box, every edge between them is drawn in one pixel or two, and no
# edge runs straight for more than three pixels. That pinch is the whole trick: it is what says
# the material is under tension instead of being a printed square.
#
# The weave is still faked with a two-tone checker whose tones sit close together, so it reads
# as texture rather than as stripes, with the lit edge along the top-left and the shadow
# pooling under the lower-right.

@palette canvas
. = none
# outline, a desaturated brown-green so it reads as undyed hemp and not as leather. Two tones,
# because the linter checks the *outline itself* for lighting direction: O is the lit upper-left
# edge, o the shaded lower-right one.
O = 5A5638
o = 262417
# the two weave tones
a = A39C6A
b = 827B4E
# lit fold along the top-left edge -- items are lit from the top left, so the highlight lives
# there and the shadow opposite. Getting this backwards is the single thing the texture linter
# catches most often, and it makes an item look inside-out at a glance.
h = D6CB90
# shadow where the cloth falls away under the lower-right edge
s = 5F5A3B

@texture item/hemp_canvas 16x16 type=item palette=canvas
